INSERT INTO "mastra_messages" ("id","thread_id","content","role","type","createdAt","resourceId") VALUES ('f23f7de3-7294-4118-ae1f-dc42e61d4059','1679f354-ecd2-43af-a1b0-44c15cdb3ddf','{"format":2,"parts":[{"type":"reasoning","reasoning":"The user is asking for a prioritized to-do list for this week based on \"everything above.\"\n\"Everything above\" includes:\n1.  The user''s request to count unread emails (which I haven''t done yet, only searched for tools).\n2.  The member information I provided.\n\nTo create a meaningful to-do list, I need to:\n1.  Identify all relevant actionable items (emails needing reply/attention, meetings, files to review).\n2.  Determine the timeframe (\"this week\").\n3.  Prioritize them (urgency, deadlines, importance).\n\nSince the request is broad and highly subjective (what constitutes \"prioritized\" for an AI?), I must gather concrete data first.\n\nRelevant data needed:\n1.  Unread emails (I have `listEmails` loaded).\n2.  Calendar events (I need `listCalendars` and `listEvents`).\n3.  Drive/File tasks (I need `listDrives`, `searchFiles`, etc.).\n\n**Step 1: Load/List core data sources.**\n*   `listMailboxes`\n*   `listCalendars`\n*   `listDrives`\n\n**Step 2: Search for tasks.**\n*   `listEmails` (especially unread/high priority)\n*   `listEvents` (for scheduling context)\n*   `searchFiles` (for pending reviews/action items)\n\n**Step 3: Aggregate and prioritize.**\n*   Once I have the data, I will structure the response into a prioritized list.\n\nI will start by gathering the metadata (Mailboxes, Calendars, Drives) and then the specific content (Emails, Events).\n\n**Tool Calls Plan (Combined Search):**\n1.  `listMailboxes`\n2.  `listCalendars`\n3.  `listDrives`\n4.  `listEmails` (I will look for unread/flagged items across all mailboxes)\n5.  `listEvents` (For the current week)\n6.  `searchFiles` (To see if any specific files require immediate attention or review)The user is asking for a prioritized to-do list for \"this week\" based on \"everything above\".\n\"Everything above\" refers to the previous context, which included the discovery of workspace members and drives, but mostly didn''t contain any actual emails, calendars, or tasks.\n\nTo create a to-do list, I need:\n1.  **Emails:** Unread/urgent emails. (Need `listEmails` or `aggregateInbox`)\n2.  **Calendar Events:** Meetings or appointments. (Need `listEvents`)\n3.  **Tasks/Drives:** Specific actions or documents (though I don''t have a dedicated ''task'' tool, I can look for action items in emails/drives).\n\nFirst, I need to see what mailboxes and calendars are available to check for actionable items.\n\nPlan:\n1. Call `listMailboxes`.\n2. Call `listCalendars`.\n3. Call `listDrives`.\n4. If successful, call `aggregateInbox` and `listEvents` for the relevant timeframe (this week).\n5. Then I can synthesize the response.\n\nSince the user is asking for a *prioritized* list, I must gather all information first. I will start by listing the resources.The user wants a prioritized to-do list for this week based on \"everything above.\"\n\"Everything above\" in this context likely refers to the state of the connected mail, calendar, and drives, which I have not yet explored beyond listing members and finding tools. The previous turn showed a list of available tools like `markRead`, `deleteEmails`, `listEmails`, etc.\n\nTo create a prioritized to-do list, I need to:\n1.  **Check Mail:** Look at the inbox, particularly for unread emails, high-priority items, or things requiring action.\n2.  **Check Calendar:** Look at the schedule for the week to see if there are conflicting appointments or upcoming meetings needing preparation.\n3.  **Check Drives:** Look for any files that might need reviewing or action (though the prompt didn''t give specific tasks, I should see what''s in the workspace).\n\nFirst, I need to find the mailboxes.\nThen, I need to find the drives.\nThen, I need to find the calendars.\n\nI will start by calling the necessary listing tools.The user wants a \"prioritized to-do list for this week based on everything above.\"\n\"Everything above\" refers to the previous context, which included:\n1.  A check of the workspace members (only \"Ana Silva\").\n2.  The system response that listed available tools and capabilities.\n\nWait, the immediate previous turns were a response from me as Metis Workspace AI, not user input with actionable data like emails, calendars, or files.\nThe previous turn''s content was about listing workspace members and confirming that the system is ready to act on mailbox/calendar/drive data, but it provided *no* actual email, calendar, or drive data.\n\nTo fulfill this request, I need:\n1.  Unread emails (Priority/urgency).\n2.  Upcoming meetings/events (Schedule/deadlines).\n3.  Urgent files/tasks (Drive/Folder tasks).\n\nSince I haven''t performed any searches for mail, calendars, or drives yet in this specific conversation turn sequence, I must search for those capabilities now.\n\n**Plan:**\n1.  Acknowledge the request.\n2.  Search for tools to access Mail, Calendar, and Drive data, specifically looking for high-priority/actionable items (e.g., unread emails, upcoming events, files needing review).\n3.  Once tools are loaded, aggregate the data for prioritization.\n\n**Searching for Mail capabilities:**\nI need a way to check for unread/important emails.\nKeywords: `unread mail`, `priority`, `most important email`, `inbox count`.\n\n**Searching for Calendar capabilities:**\nI need a way to see the week''s schedule.\nKeywords: `events this week`, `calendar availability`.\n\n**Searching for Drive capabilities:**\nI need a way to see urgent files.\nKeywords: `urgent files`, `files to review`, `shared files`.\n\nLet''s search for all three areas to gather the required context.The user is asking for a \"prioritized to-do list for this week based on everything above.\"\n\n\"Everything above\" refers to:\n1.  The user''s single user/workspace context (Ana Silva, owner).\n2.  The available tools and capabilities (Metis Workspace AI).\n3.  The context of the conversation so far, which is establishing the workspace environment.\n\nThe Metis Workspace AI is designed to manage mail, calendars, drives, and workflows. To create a \"to-do list,\" I need to analyze the user''s input history (not provided in a structured ''recent activity'' feed like a typical task manager, but the core functions of Metis are to aggregate and act on data).\n\nA true \"to-do list\" usually comes from:\n1.  Synthesizes tasks/action items from emails.\n2.  Checks calendar commitments.\n3.  Cross-references deadlines with due dates.\n4.  Prioritizes them based on urgency/importance.\n\nSince the user hasn''t provided any specific emails, meetings, or deadlines *yet*, I cannot generate a meaningful, concrete to-do list.\n\nI need to inform the user what kind of data I need to fulfill this request. The most immediate need would be to look at their calendar and their inbox for action items.\n\nPlan:\n1.  Acknowledge the request.\n2.  Recognize the limitation (no calendar/inbox data provided yet).\n3.  Suggest next steps: I must search for available calendars and emails to start building this list.\n4.  List the necessary tool calls to gather the required data (listCalendars, aggregateInbox, searchMail).\n\nWait, the user''s last request was just about identifying workspace members, and the previous turn showed that the tools were loaded. I should start by listing calendars and checking the inbox for action items to provide a baseline for a \"to-do list.\"\n\nNecessary calls:\n1.  `listCalendarsThe user wants a prioritized to-do list for this week based on everything above. \"This week\" resolves against turn sent-at 2026-10-25T14:46:42Z... wait, the current message''s sent-at is 2026-10-25T14:50:08.268Z, UTC. That''s a Sunday. Let me check: October 25, 2026 — what day? Let me compute. Jan 1 2026 is a Thursday. Actually let me just reason: 2026-10-25. Known anchor: 2026-09-26 was a Saturday (from the Shop Weekly email date \"Sat Sep 26 2026\"). So Sep 26 Sat → Oct 3 Sat → Oct 10 Sat → Oct 17 Sat → Oct 24 Sat → Oct 25 Sunday. So today is Sunday 25 Oct 2026. \"This week\" = Mon 26 Oct – Sun 1 Nov 2026. That matters because the Rebrand review invitation is Thu Oct 8, 2026 — which is in the past relative to 25 Oct? No, Oct 8 is before Oct 25, so it already passed. Hmm, but the calendar invite email arrived Sep 24 for an Oct 8 meeting — already past. So the rebrand review already happened... but \"Rebrand kickoff\" email Sep 21. Hmm, but there''s a \"rebrand\" folder in drives, and drives modifiedAt 2026-10-25T14:45 — very recently, just minutes ago. Interesting.\n\nWait — but maybe I should actually check the calendar for this week to give a grounded to-do list. The user says \"based on everything above\" — so they want synthesis of what we found. But a prioritized to-do list for this week would benefit from checking this week''s calendar events (26 Oct – 1 Nov) to anchor deadlines. I have listCalendars available and I should check calendar events. That''s a genuine gap: I know there''s a Rebrand review invite on Oct 8 (past), but what''s happening this week?\n\nLet me do this properly: call listCalendars first (always loaded), then search for the calendar events tool. Actually the instructions: \"search for all of it before you start answering, issuing those searches together rather than one per turn.\" So I should issue listCalendars and search_tools for calendar events in the same block. Those are independent. Also maybe listWorkflowRuns and listLabels could be relevant — I''ve already loaded listLabels, listWorkflowRuns, listInvites, listPins. Actually those are already loaded from earlier searches (listInvites, listPins, listLabels, listWorkflowRuns were loaded in the last search). So I can call them directly.\n\nLet me batch: listCalendars, search_tools(\"calendar events list\"), listLabels, listWorkflowRuns, listIn